Document.description_en:
The Lorneville Small Craft Harbour (SCH) is situated on a natural promontory extending northward into Lorneville Harbour.&nbsp; The harbour is further protected by a 165 m-long rubblemound breakwater (SCH Facility Number (#) 301) that extends out from the headland.&nbsp; The existing public wharf (#401) is a tee-shaped timber pile structure located on the lee side of the breakwater.&nbsp; The structure consists of square-post trestle bents along the approach stem and a timber pile bent system at the tee section.&nbsp; The wharf is more than 60 years old and has reached the end of its service life.

&nbsp;

The proposed works will involve the complete demolition and removal of the wharf structure.&nbsp; Demolition activities will likely be undertaken using land-based construction methods, either from a temporary earthen platform constructed along the interior side of the existing breakwater (#301) or by installation of a temporary trestle bridge.&nbsp; These approaches will facilitate the use of land-based equipment, including long-reach excavators, cranes, and haul trucks operating in dry conditions.&nbsp; Alternatively, the methodology could also involve a barge operation with equipment such as a crane and/or excavator operating from the floating platform to dismantle the wharf and relay material to the upland.

&nbsp;

The existing structure will likely be dismantled in sections or segments and temporarily placed along the approach road for further processing and separation.&nbsp; Materials suitable for recycling or reuse will be salvaged where practical, while unsuitable materials will be managed as waste, loaded onto trucks, and transported to provincially-approved disposal facilities.&nbsp; Demolition activities will also involve the removal of ballast boxes and stone and may also require incidental excavation of underwater sediments.&nbsp; Following completion of demolition activities, all temporary work areas and disturbed ground surfaces will be stabilized as required, with ballast and earthen materials/rock from temporary access reworked into the existing breakwater.&nbsp; All construction equipment and construction waste materials will be removed from the site.

&nbsp;

Demolition activities are anticipated to commence in November 2026 and be completed by the end of March 2027.&nbsp; Project commencement is, however, subject to DFO-SCH operational priorities and funding, as well as the completion of regulatory approval processes, therefore work may be delayed.&nbsp; The scope of the environmental effects determination (subject to S.82 of the Impact Assessment Act) will, therefore, be based on the works being completed over a period of three (3) months commencing within a period of 5 years following the determination of significance of environmental effects.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

The Lorneville SCH is fishing harbour that was originally developed to serve the fishing industry in the area.&nbsp; The site has been used as a fishing harbour for several decades. Departmental records indicate that a breakwater has been in place since at least the late 1800s.&nbsp; Its well-protected location provides easy access to the fishing grounds of the Bay of Fundy.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Given that the existing wharf is well beyond its service life and load restricted, the status quo of leaving the deteriorated structures available and in service represents a contingent liability to the Crown.&nbsp; Repairs are not practical and complete reconstruction would be necessary to provide the same level of service.

&nbsp;

The four (4) homeport vessels at the Lorneville SCH have relocated to the Five Fathom Harbour SCH (approximately 8.5 kms to the northwest) due to the harbour&apos;s deteriorated condition.&nbsp; Following review of the costs associated with reconstruction of the wharf at Lorneville, DFO-SCH decided that permanent accommodation of these users at Five Fathom Harbour SCH through the installation of an additional floating dock was the most practicable and cost-effective long-term option.

Document.description_fr:
Le Port pour petits bateaux (PPB) de Lorneville est situ&eacute; sur un promontoire naturel s&apos;&eacute;tendant vers le nord dans le havre de Lorneville. Le port est en outre prot&eacute;g&eacute; par une jet&eacute;e en enrochement de 165 m de longueur (installation PPB num&eacute;ro (no.) 301) qui s&apos;avance depuis le cap. &nbsp;Le quai public existant (no. 401) est une structure en forme de &laquo; T &raquo; compos&eacute;e de pieux de bois, situ&eacute;e du c&ocirc;t&eacute; sous le vent de la jet&eacute;e. &nbsp;La structure comprend des trav&eacute;es sur pieux carr&eacute;s le long de la section d&apos;acc&egrave;s et un syst&egrave;me de pieux de bois &agrave; la section en T. &nbsp;Le quai a plus de 60 ans et a atteint la fin de sa vie utile.

&nbsp;

Les travaux propos&eacute;s consisteront en la d&eacute;molition compl&egrave;te et l&apos;enl&egrave;vement de la structure du quai. Les activit&eacute;s de d&eacute;molition seront vraisemblablement r&eacute;alis&eacute;es &agrave; partir de la terre ferme, soit depuis une plateforme temporaire en remblai construite le long du c&ocirc;t&eacute; int&eacute;rieur de la jet&eacute;e existante (no. 301), soit au moyen de l&apos;installation d&apos;un pont-tr&eacute;teau temporaire. Ces approches permettront l&apos;utilisation d&apos;&eacute;quipement terrestre, notamment des excavatrices &agrave; longue port&eacute;e, des grues et des camions de transport op&eacute;rant en conditions s&egrave;ches. &nbsp;&Agrave; titre alternatif, la m&eacute;thodologie pourrait &eacute;galement inclure une op&eacute;ration &agrave; partir d&apos;une barge &eacute;quip&eacute;e d&apos;une grue et/ou d&apos;une excavatrice pour d&eacute;manteler le quai et transf&eacute;rer les mat&eacute;riaux vers la terre ferme.

&nbsp;

La structure existante sera vraisemblablement d&eacute;mont&eacute;e par sections ou segments et temporairement d&eacute;pos&eacute;e le long de la route d&apos;acc&egrave;s pour traitement et tri. Les mat&eacute;riaux pouvant &ecirc;tre recycl&eacute;s ou r&eacute;utilis&eacute;s seront r&eacute;cup&eacute;r&eacute;s lorsque possible, tandis que les mat&eacute;riaux non r&eacute;utilisables seront g&eacute;r&eacute;s comme d&eacute;chets, charg&eacute;s dans des camions et transport&eacute;s vers des installations d&apos;&eacute;limination approuv&eacute;es par la province. Les activit&eacute;s de d&eacute;molition comprendront &eacute;galement l&apos;enl&egrave;vement des caissons de lest et de la pierre, et pourraient n&eacute;cessiter une excavation accessoire des s&eacute;diments sous-marins. Une fois les travaux de d&eacute;molition termin&eacute;s, toutes les zones de travail temporaires et les surfaces de sol perturb&eacute;es seront stabilis&eacute;es au besoin, et les mat&eacute;riaux de lest, de remblai ou de roche provenant des acc&egrave;s temporaires seront r&eacute;int&eacute;gr&eacute;s &agrave; la jet&eacute;e existante. Tout l&apos;&eacute;quipement de construction et les mat&eacute;riaux r&eacute;siduels seront retir&eacute;s du site.

&nbsp;

Les activit&eacute;s de d&eacute;molition devraient commencer en novembre 2026 et se terminer d&apos;ici la fin de mars 2027. Toutefois, le d&eacute;but du projet demeure tributaire des priorit&eacute;s op&eacute;rationnelles et du financement de MPO-PPB, ainsi que de l&apos;obtention des autorisations r&eacute;glementaires; par cons&eacute;quent, les travaux pourraient &ecirc;tre retard&eacute;s. La port&eacute;e de la d&eacute;termination des effets environnementaux (en vertu de l&apos;article 82 de la Loi sur l&apos;&eacute;valuation d&apos;impact) sera donc fond&eacute;e sur l&apos;hypoth&egrave;se que les travaux seront r&eacute;alis&eacute;s sur une p&eacute;riode de trois (3) mois, et ce, dans un d&eacute;lai maximal de cinq ans suivant la d&eacute;termination de l&apos;importance des effets environnementaux.

&nbsp;

Le PPB de Lorneville est un port de p&ecirc;che initialement am&eacute;nag&eacute; pour desservir l&apos;industrie de la p&ecirc;che locale. Le site est utilis&eacute; comme port de p&ecirc;che depuis plusieurs d&eacute;cennies. Les dossiers minist&eacute;riels indiquent qu&apos;une jet&eacute;e est en place depuis au moins la fin des ann&eacute;es 1800. Son emplacement bien prot&eacute;g&eacute; offre un acc&egrave;s facile aux zones de p&ecirc;che de la baie de Fundy.

&nbsp;

&Eacute;tant donn&eacute; que le quai existant a largement d&eacute;pass&eacute; sa vie utile et qu&apos;il est soumis &agrave; des restrictions de charge, le maintien du statu quo &mdash; soit laisser en place des structures d&eacute;t&eacute;rior&eacute;es &mdash; repr&eacute;sente un passif &eacute;ventuel pour l&apos;&Eacute;tat. Les r&eacute;parations ne sont pas pratiques, et une reconstruction compl&egrave;te serait n&eacute;cessaire pour offrir le m&ecirc;me niveau de service.

&nbsp;

Les quatre (4) navires bas&eacute;s au PPB de Lorneville ont relocalis&eacute;s au PPB de Five Fathom Harbour (situ&eacute; &agrave; environ 8,5 km au nord-ouest) en raison de la d&eacute;t&eacute;rioration du port. Apr&egrave;s l&apos;examen des co&ucirc;ts associ&eacute;s &agrave; la reconstruction du quai &agrave; Lorneville, MPO-PPB a d&eacute;termin&eacute; que l&apos;h&eacute;bergement permanent de ces utilisateurs au PPB de Five Fathom Harbour, gr&acirc;ce &agrave; l&apos;installation d&apos;un quai flottant suppl&eacute;mentaire, constituait l&apos;option la plus pratique et la plus rentable &agrave; long terme.

